{"name":"Etruscan Bronze","hex":"#bb986f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bronze","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A naturally occurring or man-made alloy of copper, usually in combination with tin, but also with one or more other metals.","A reddish-brown colour, the colour of bronze."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of bronze metal.","Having a reddish-brown colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bronze"}],"entries":[{"title":"Etruscan civilization","blocks":[{"text":"The Etruscans created a civilization in Etruria, in ancient Italy, with a common language and culture, and formed a federation of city-states. After adjacent lands had been conquered, its territory covered, at its greatest extent, roughly what is now Tuscany, western Umbria and northern Lazio, as well as what are now the Po Valley, Emilia-Romagna, south-eastern Lombardy, southern Veneto and western Campania."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Etruscan_civilization_map.png/330px-Etruscan_civilization_map.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etruscan_civilization"},{"title":"Bronze","blocks":[{"text":"Bronze is an alloy consisting primarily of copper, commonly with about 12–12.5% tin and often with the addition of other metals and sometimes non-metals or metalloids. These additions produce a range of alloys some of which are harder than copper alone or have other useful properties, such as strength, ductility, or machinability."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Bull%27s_head_ornament_for_a_lyre_MET_DP260070.jpg/330px-Bull%27s_head_ornament_for_a_lyre_MET_DP260070.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bronze"},{"title":"Bronze (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Bronze is a metallic brown color which resembles the metal alloy bronze. 